WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.265
KEITH DANEKIND: Hi,
I'm Keith Danekind,

00:00:02.265 --> 00:00:04.223
and I'm a customer engineer
on the Apigee team.

00:00:04.223 --> 00:00:05.682
Today I want to
show you how Apigee

00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:07.870
can help modernize a legacy
application to enable

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.850
new digital experiences.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.350
We'll look at a
demo where I'm going

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.990
to move a service from a
legacy environment to the cloud

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.270
without disruption and add
a bunch of other benefits

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:17.540
along the way.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.480
Here's a diagram that
describes the problem

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:20.733
I'm trying to solve.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.900
There's a service running
in the legacy environment,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.060
and I'd like to move
it into Google Cloud.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.190
I'd like to do this without
disrupting the existing

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.620
applications and establish
a pattern that I can

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.110
use for moving future services.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.510
I'll start by building
a facade in Apigee.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.280
I'll make well-designed
REST services that

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.380
have consistent
interfaces and security,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.350
and I'll map those to
the back-end services.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.970
I'll map the basic
CRUD operations--

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.370
Create, Read, Update,
and Delete-- to the HTTP

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.010
verbs POST, GET,
PUT, and DELETE.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.140
I'll use Apigee's routing
features to route traffic

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.910
to the right back-end
services, and I'll

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.580
use Apigee to manage API
keys for each application

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.030
and create policies in Apigee
to manage the flow of traffic.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.753
Here's the goals I
have for my project.

00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:01.420
First, I want to move
the legacy service

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.520
to the cloud with no downtime.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.890
I want to start by testing
the cloud service by routing

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.320
10% of the traffic to the
cloud, make sure everything's

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.470
working the right way.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.470
I want to get visibility
into which applications

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:12.598
are using the service.

00:01:12.598 --> 00:01:14.140
Today there's a
single URL, and we've

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.620
got some logs and
things like that,

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.590
but I don't know which
application is sending

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:18.990
in traffic specifically.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.370
I'll add API keys in
Apigee that'll identify

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.008
individual applications.

00:01:23.008 --> 00:01:25.300
I want to add some rate limits
to protect the services.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.550
We don't want one
application calling too much.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.400
We'll add call quotas based
on the partner relationship,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.527
so different partners
get different quotas.

00:01:32.527 --> 00:01:35.110
I want to remove some sensitive
internal data from the partner

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:36.880
applications.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.130
I need to add support
for XML-formatted data

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.840
to support some of
the legacy clients.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.750
If they request XML,
we'll return it.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.000
I want to make it easy to
onboard new applications

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.460
in the future and get ready
for the hundreds of partner

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.133
applications I know are coming.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:51.550
And throughout all
of this, I want

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.300
to establish a
reference architecture

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.110
so that this can be the pattern
for moving future services.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.710
Let's look at a demo and see
how to build this in Apigee.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.110
Here in Apigee, I've configured
two target endpoints,

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.150
one for each of the
different back-end systems,

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.940
the legacy system
and the cloud system.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.440
I also have a URL
here where it will

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.190
be listening for traffic
to come in and be

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.240
routed through Apigee.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.842
This is where I manage the
request and response flow.

00:02:14.842 --> 00:02:16.300
I'll put different
policies in here

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.510
to affect how the traffic flows.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.490
The first one checks
for an API key.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.770
Next is a Spike Arrest to
prevent too much traffic

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.150
from coming in.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.710
Then there's a
business-related quota

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.418
that's going to limit
the number of calls

00:02:28.418 --> 00:02:29.782
depending on who's calling.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.990
The next two are what we're
going to use specifically

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:33.280
for routing.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.680
Apigee has a key value
map, and inside of there I

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.433
store important values.

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:39.100
I've put a probability
in there that I'm

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.070
going to use to determine how
much traffic goes to the cloud

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:44.290
and how much goes to
the legacy system.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.540
So this looks up
that value, and then

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.000
we're going to have some
JavaScript in here to randomly

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.507
set a flag, and that
flag will determine

00:02:51.507 --> 00:02:53.840
whether we get routed to the
cloud or the legacy system.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.810
Let's look at how that works.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.150
In this JavaScript
policy, we see there's

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.018
some code referenced here.

00:03:01.018 --> 00:03:03.060
So here's our code for
generating a random number

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.250
and then checking to see
if it's past the threshold

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.640
and setting a flag
to true if it is.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:11.700
So testGroup will be set to
true if we're in the test group.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.182
Down here we have
some route rules.

00:03:13.182 --> 00:03:15.390
Route rules let us configure
where the traffic should

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:16.490
be routed in Apigee.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.240
You can have multiple
different back ends,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.040
and you can route to different
ones using some of these rules.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.710
I've got two back-end
systems in here.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.740
I have the cloud system
and the legacy system.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.942
If the condition
testGroup equals to true,

00:03:27.942 --> 00:03:29.400
this rule will be
true and we'll be

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.800
routed to the cloud back end.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.790
That gives us a 10%
chance, in this case,

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.750
of being routed to cloud.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.500
There are a few more
goals I had for this,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.050
and we can see those
on the response side.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.310
One goal was to
remove sensitive data,

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:46.200
so there's a particular field in
there that I want to take out.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.630
I've got some JavaScript in
here to remove a sales group,

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.120
so there's a particular
field called SalesGroup.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.780
I want to delete that from
the JSON that comes back.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.530
If there's an accept
header on the request

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.160
equal to application/XML,
this policy will fire,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.200
and it'll convert
from JSON to XML

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.300
and return the
data in XML format

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.310
to those clients who need it.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.040
I can build a custom
report in Apigee

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.457
to see how much traffic is
going to the legacy environment

00:04:10.457 --> 00:04:13.210
and how much is
going to the cloud.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.105
I can click through on each
metric and get more details.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.573
This shows me traffic from
each of the applications,

00:04:21.573 --> 00:04:22.990
and I get an idea
of who's sending

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.718
the traffic into the system.

00:04:24.718 --> 00:04:26.260
One of the other
goals was to make it

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.750
easy to onboard new partners.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.085
I've created a developer
portal in Apigee,

00:04:31.085 --> 00:04:32.710
and this provides
complete self-service

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.668
for people who want to
write applications using

00:04:34.668 --> 00:04:36.093
the APIs that I've provided.

00:04:36.093 --> 00:04:37.010
They can come in here.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.622
They can read documentation
about how the APIs work.

00:04:39.622 --> 00:04:41.080
They can try them
out, and they can

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.443
sign up and get credentials.

00:04:43.443 --> 00:04:44.860
That was a quick
demo about how we

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.230
might use Apigee to
modernize a legacy service

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:49.450
and move it up to the cloud,
and add a bunch of stuff

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:50.630
along the way.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:51.730
Thanks for watching today.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:55.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

